# Writing Plan Markdown

Plans use CommonMark with the GitHub extensions: tables, task lists, strikethrough, and autolinks. CoPlan also supports the features on this page, and slide decks inside a plan (see <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions/presentations>). CoPlan removes all other raw HTML when it shows the plan, but keeps it in the source.

This guide assumes that you have read the primer at <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions>.

## Tables

Use a standard pipe table when each item has two or more attributes:

```markdown
| Region | p95 latency | Owner |
|--------|------------:|-------|
| us-east | 180 ms | Payments |
| eu-west | 240 ms | Platform |
```

- Colons in the separator row set column alignment.
- Keep cells short. Put explanations in a paragraph or a footnote, not in a cell.
- Escape a literal pipe in a cell as `\|`.

## Section links

Number the headings that you refer to, and link to them with an explicit fragment. A heading `### 3.1 Rollout` has the fragment `#section-3-1`:

```markdown
The flag stays off until the checks in [§3.1](#section-3-1) pass.
```

Readers see a preview of the section on hover. CoPlan does not link a bare `§3.1`, because plans also use `§` to cite external documents such as laws.

## Footnotes and links

- **To support a claim, use a footnote.** Put the source link and the supporting detail in the definition: `Queue depth peaked at 40k.[^queue-depth]` and `[^queue-depth]: Measured during the 2026-08-12 incident. [Dashboard](https://example.com/d/queues).` Readers can preview the note on hover. Use a descriptive footnote name, not a number.
- **To point to a resource, use a descriptive link:** `[implementation PR](https://github.com/org/repo/pull/42)`. Do not put bare URLs in prose.
- **To give a link a stable name, use a reference-style link:** `See [auth-repo].` with `[auth-repo]: https://github.com/org/auth-service "Auth Service"`. The key and title go to the plan's References.

CoPlan adds every link in the content, including links in footnotes, to the plan's References section. Do not add these links again through the API. See "References" in <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions/creating>.

## Diagrams

Write Mermaid in a `mermaid` fence. CoPlan shows the diagram, and the source stays editable with the rest of the plan:

````markdown
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Client --> API
  API --> Database
```
````

Use a diagram for architecture, data flow, sequences, or state. Do not use a diagram when a short list says the same thing. Readers can comment on a node or an edge of a diagram, and on a cell of a table.

## Code blocks

Always put a language tag on a code fence. CoPlan highlights the syntax with highlight.js and accepts any language name that highlight.js supports. Use the canonical names: `ruby`, `python`, `javascript`, `typescript`, `go`, `java`, `kotlin`, `swift`, `rust`, `c`, `cpp`, `csharp`, `sql`, `json`, `yaml`, `xml` (also for HTML), `css`, `graphql`, `protobuf`, `bash` (scripts), `shell` (terminal sessions with `$` prompts), `diff`, `http`. Use `plaintext` for logs. Do not invent tags: CoPlan shows an unknown tag as plain text.

## Task lists

`- [ ] Item` and `- [x] Done item` render as checkboxes. Readers can select a checkbox in the web UI. **Each selection is a hand edit.** After a person selects a checkbox, CoPlan refuses your next write until you read the plan again.

## Collapsible sections

Use `<details>` for long appendices, raw logs, or optional detail:

```markdown
<details><summary>Raw benchmark output</summary>

Markdown here still renders. Keep the blank line after the summary.

</details>
```

Add `open` to the `<details>` tag to show the section expanded by default.

## Hover definitions

Use `<abbr title="Point of sale">POS</abbr>` for acronyms and team terms. Readers see the definition on hover.

## Images and attachments

Upload a file to the plan, then paste the `markdown` value from the response into the content. Images show in the plan body.

```bash
<%= @curl %> -X POST -F "file=@./architecture.png" \
  "<%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/attachments" | jq -r .markdown
```

- The maximum size is 25 MB per file. Allowed types are PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, PDF, TXT, MD, CSV, JSON, and ZIP. CoPlan returns `422` for other files.
- Only the plan author can upload or delete attachments. Any reader can list and download them.
- List the attachments: `GET <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/attachments`. Delete one: `DELETE <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/attachments/$ATTACHMENT_ID`.
- If a Mermaid diagram can show the content, use Mermaid instead of an image. A person can edit Mermaid; nobody can edit an image of a diagram.
